## 7.5 Module 5: Character-by-Character Typing Engine

### Purpose
Simulate human-like keyboard input into a target application window, replacing the disruptive instant-paste workflow.

| Req ID | Requirement | Priority | Acceptance Criteria |
|---|---|---|---|
| FR-TY-01 | The typing engine MUST send individual keystrokes to the currently focused window using the Windows `SendInput` API | P0 | Characters appear one at a time in the target application |
| FR-TY-02 | The typing engine MUST support 10 speed levels | P0 | See speed calibration table below |
| FR-TY-03 | The typing engine MUST support pausing in-progress typing | P0 | Pause freezes at the current character; no characters are dropped |
| FR-TY-04 | The typing engine MUST support resuming paused typing | P0 | Resume continues from the exact paused position |
| FR-TY-05 | The typing engine MUST support cancelling in-progress typing | P0 | Cancel stops immediately; remaining text is not typed; queue is preserved |
| FR-TY-06 | The typing engine MUST support a queue of snippets to type sequentially | P1 | Queue panel shows order, snippet preview, and estimated time |
| FR-TY-07 | The typing engine MUST display a progress indicator (percentage complete) | P1 | Progress bar updates in real time |
| FR-TY-08 | The typing engine MUST display an estimated time remaining | P1 | ETA recalculated based on current speed |
| FR-TY-09 | The typing engine MUST support full Unicode, including multi-byte characters, emoji, CJK, RTL, and diacritics | P0 | All code points type correctly; verified with Arabic, Chinese, Japanese, Korean, and emoji test sets |
| FR-TY-10 | The typing engine MUST add human-like timing variance (±15% random jitter per keystroke) to avoid perfectly uniform intervals | P1 | Timing analysis of 1,000 keystrokes shows non-uniform distribution |
| FR-TY-11 | The typing engine MUST NOT interfere with the user's ability to use the keyboard during typing (user input takes priority) | P0 | If user presses a key during typing, the typed key is not swallowed |
| FR-TY-12 | The typing engine MUST handle newlines correctly (Enter key simulation) | P0 | Multi-line text types with correct line breaks |
| FR-TY-13 | The typing engine MUST handle tab characters (Tab key simulation) | P1 | Tabs type correctly in code editors |
| FR-TY-14 | The typing engine MUST provide a configurable delay before starting (default: 3 seconds) to allow user to focus the target window | P0 | Countdown displayed in the UI; user can adjust 1–10 seconds |

### Speed Calibration Table

| Level | Characters Per Minute | Delay Between Chars (ms) | Use Case |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | ~30 | ~2000 | Dramatic effect, accessibility |
| 2 | ~60 | ~1000 | Slow explanation |
| 3 | ~100 | ~600 | Deliberate typing |
| 4 | ~150 | ~400 | Normal human typing |
| 5 | ~200 | ~300 | Fast human typing |
| 6 | ~300 | ~200 | Efficient transfer |
| 7 | ~450 | ~133 | Quick transfer |
| 8 | ~600 | ~100 | Rapid transfer |
| 9 | ~800 | ~75 | Very fast transfer |
| 10 | ~1000 | ~60 | Maximum speed |

> [!NOTE]
> The ±15% jitter (FR-TY-10) applies on top of the base delay. For example, at speed 4 (400 ms base), actual delays will range from 340–460 ms per character.

### Implementation Guidance

The engine MUST use the Windows `SendInput` API (via `ctypes` or `pywin32`) and NOT deprecated methods like `keybd_event` or `SendKeys`. `SendInput` is the modern, supported API for synthesizing keyboard input and does not require elevated privileges.

For Unicode characters outside the basic ASCII range, the engine MUST use `KEYEVENTF_UNICODE` flag with `SendInput` to send the character directly without requiring a virtual key code mapping.

The typing engine runs on a **dedicated background thread** with a cancellation event. The main UI thread communicates via signals/slots (Qt) to update progress and handle pause/resume/cancel commands.

### Design Rationale
Character-by-character typing is the core value proposition. `SendInput` is the correct API — it is standard, documented, works without admin privileges, and is the same API used by Windows on-screen keyboard and accessibility tools. The jitter feature prevents robotic uniformity, which is important for presentation authenticity.

