## 7.10 Module 10: Session Management

### Purpose
Protect user work from loss due to crashes, unexpected shutdowns, or accidental closure.

| Req ID | Requirement | Priority | Acceptance Criteria |
|---|---|---|---|
| FR-SM-01 | The application MUST auto-save the current session at a configurable interval (default: 60 s) | P0 | Session file updated on disk every interval |
| FR-SM-02 | The application MUST save the session on graceful exit (window close, system shutdown) | P0 | Session file is up-to-date when process terminates normally |
| FR-SM-03 | The application MUST detect an ungraceful shutdown (crash) and offer session recovery on next launch | P0 | "Restore previous session?" dialog on launch if crash detected |
| FR-SM-04 | The session MUST include: workspace states, window geometry, active workspace, typing queue state | P0 | All state is restored on recovery |
| FR-SM-05 | The application MUST support manual session backup (export to file) | P2 | Full session export in a versioned archive format |
| FR-SM-06 | The application MUST support session import from a backup file | P2 | Validates integrity; warns about version mismatch |

### Crash Detection Strategy
Use a PID lock file (`data/session.lock`). On startup:
1. Check if `session.lock` exists.
2. If it exists, check if the PID in the file is still running.
3. If the PID is not running → previous instance crashed → offer recovery.
4. If the PID is running → another instance is active → warn user.
5. Write current PID to `session.lock`.
6. Delete `session.lock` on graceful exit.

