## 7.14 Module 14: Error Handling

### Purpose
Define error categories, user-facing messages, and recovery strategies.

| Error Category | Examples | User Impact | Recovery Strategy |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Network Failure** | DNS resolution failure, timeout, SSL error | Browser pages don't load | Show browser-native error page; retry button; offline indicator in status bar |
| **Browser Crash** | Tab crash, renderer process OOM | Tab content lost | Detect via `QWebEnginePage.renderProcessTerminated`; offer tab reload; log crash |
| **Typing Interruption** | Target window closed, focus lost, user locked screen | Typing paused mid-word | Auto-pause when target window is gone; notify user; resume when target returns |
| **Clipboard Error** | SQLite write failure, disk full | Entry not saved | Retry 3x; notify user; queue in memory until disk available |
| **Session Corruption** | Malformed JSON, incomplete write | Session not restorable | Fall back to last known good session; create backup of corrupted file for diagnostics |
| **Configuration Error** | Invalid settings value, schema migration failure | Settings reset | Validate on load; fall back to defaults for invalid values; warn user |
| **Hotkey Conflict** | Another app holds the hotkey | Hotkey doesn't work | Non-blocking notification; suggest alternative key combination |
| **Disk Space** | < 50 MB free on data drive | Data not saved | Warning notification; disable auto-save until space is freed |
| **Unexpected Shutdown** | Power loss, BSOD, force kill | All in-memory state lost | PID lock file detection on restart; offer session recovery |

### Error Display Philosophy

| Severity | Display Method | User Action Required |
|---|---|---|
| Info | Status bar message (fades after 5 s) | None |
| Warning | Non-blocking toast notification | Optional |
| Error | Modal dialog with explanation and action buttons | Yes |
| Critical | Modal dialog; application may need restart | Yes |

### Design Rationale
Users should never see raw exceptions, stack traces, or technical jargon. Every error message follows the pattern: *"What happened"* → *"Why it matters"* → *"What you can do."*

