# 12. Technology Evaluation

## 12.1 Language Selection

| Option | Pros | Cons | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Python 3.11+** | Rapid development; rich ecosystem; Pydantic for models; PyInstaller for distribution; large talent pool | Slower than compiled languages; larger binary; GIL limits CPU parallelism | ✅ **Selected** |
| C# / .NET | Native Windows; excellent UI frameworks (WPF, WinUI); fast | No mature embedded Chromium-in-WPF solution comparable to Qt WebEngine; harder to portable-deploy without runtime | ❌ |
| C++ / Qt | Maximum performance; native Qt integration | Slow development; memory management burden; smaller talent pool | ❌ |
| Electron / Node.js | Easy web UI; Chromium built-in | Very large binary (~300 MB); high RAM; not aligned with "lightweight" goal | ❌ |
| Rust / Tauri | Small binary; modern; secure | Immature GUI ecosystem; Qt WebEngine not available; high learning curve | ❌ |

**Justification:** Python offers the optimal balance of development speed, ecosystem maturity, and distribution tooling. PySide6 provides a production-grade Qt binding with integrated Chromium (Qt WebEngine). The typing engine's performance-critical path (SendInput) is offloaded to native Windows APIs via ctypes, avoiding Python's GIL limitations. PyInstaller produces a self-contained portable folder.

## 12.2 GUI Framework Selection

| Option | Pros | Cons | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| **PySide6 (Qt 6)** | Official Qt binding (LGPL); mature; Qt WebEngine (Chromium); native look; accessibility API; comprehensive widget set | Large binary (~150 MB with WebEngine); requires careful resource management | ✅ **Selected** |
| PyQt6 | Same feature set as PySide6 | GPL license (restrictive for commercial use) unless commercial license purchased | ❌ |
| Tkinter | Lightweight; stdlib | No embedded browser; dated look; limited accessibility | ❌ |
| Dear PyGui | Fast rendering; modern | No embedded browser; immature; limited widget set | ❌ |
| wxPython | Native look; CEF integration possible | CEF integration is complex; smaller community than Qt | ❌ |

**Justification:** PySide6 is the only Python GUI framework that provides an integrated, supported, production-ready Chromium browser engine (Qt WebEngine) while also offering a rich widget toolkit, accessibility APIs, styling (QSS), and a proven deployment story via PyInstaller. The LGPL license allows commercial use without restrictions.

## 12.3 Browser Engine Selection

| Option | Pros | Cons | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Qt WebEngine (Chromium)** | Integrated with PySide6; single-vendor dependency; well-documented; automatic Chromium updates via Qt releases | Large binary contribution (~100 MB); limited DevTools customization | ✅ **Selected** |
| CEF (Chromium Embedded Framework) | More control; dedicated browser embedding framework | Complex Python integration (cefpython3 is unmaintained for Python 3.11+); separate process management | ❌ |
| WebView2 (Microsoft Edge) | Small binary (Edge is pre-installed on Win 10/11); modern | No official Python binding; interop complexity with Qt UI | ❌ |
| Playwright / Selenium (headless) | Easy to automate | Not for embedding in a GUI; headless only | ❌ |

**Justification:** Qt WebEngine is the natural choice when using PySide6. It is maintained by the Qt Company, receives regular Chromium updates, and integrates seamlessly with the Qt widget tree. No interop layer is needed.

