# 15. Security Design

## 15.1 Security Principles

1. **Least privilege.** The application runs as a standard user. No admin rights, no elevated tokens.
2. **Local only.** All data stays on the user's machine. No network communication except browser traffic initiated by the user.
3. **Transparency.** The application is always visible and identifiable. It never hides its window, process name, or identity.
4. **Defense in depth.** Input validation at every boundary. Fail-safe defaults.
5. **Minimal attack surface.** No custom protocols, no listening sockets, no IPC servers.

## 15.2 Security Non-Negotiables — Explicit Confirmation

> [!CAUTION]
> The following capabilities are **permanently excluded** from the product. Any attempt to add them constitutes a security violation and must be rejected at code review.

| Excluded Capability | Reason |
|---|---|
| Hide from screen recording/sharing | Stealth behavior; associated with cheating |
| Bypass application or browser security | Illegal under CFAA and equivalents |
| Circumvent online examination systems | Unethical; legal liability |
| Evade anti-cheat or detection systems | Unethical; legal liability |
| Impersonate Windows system processes | Malware behavior; triggers antivirus |
| Inject code into other processes | Requires admin; malware behavior |
| Hook third-party application APIs | Requires admin; malware behavior |
| Manipulate memory of other processes | Requires admin; malware behavior |
| Interfere with Windows security | Illegal; malware behavior |
| Privilege escalation | Security violation |
| Install drivers or kernel components | Requires admin; stability risk |
| Modify Windows registry or internals | Incompatible with portability |

## 15.3 Secure Local Storage

| Data | Protection Mechanism |
|---|---|
| Browser cookies | Stored in Chromium's encrypted cookie store (Qt WebEngine default behavior) |
| Browser local storage | Stored in Chromium's LevelDB within the workspace's browser profile directory |
| Application settings | Unencrypted JSON (no sensitive data; user preferences only) |
| Clipboard entries | Unencrypted SQLite (user-generated content; user controls their own data) |
| Logs | No sensitive data logged (FR-LG-05, FR-LG-06); plain text |

> [!NOTE]
> **Decision: No additional encryption for clipboard data.** Clipboard entries are user-generated text that the user explicitly copies. Encrypting them would add complexity without meaningful security benefit — an attacker with file system access already has access to the Chromium profile (which contains cookies, passwords, etc.). The appropriate protection is OS-level (disk encryption, user account passwords).

## 15.4 Credential Protection

- The application **does not** store any credentials itself. All authentication is handled by the embedded browser (Chromium).
- Chromium stores credentials using its built-in credential management, which encrypts passwords using the Windows DPAPI (Data Protection API) — the same mechanism used by Chrome and Edge.
- The application **does not** expose APIs to read, export, or transmit stored browser credentials.

## 15.5 Session Protection

| Mechanism | Implementation |
|---|---|
| PID lock file | Prevents session conflict between instances |
| Atomic file writes | Prevents corruption on crash |
| Session integrity check | Validates JSON schema on load; falls back to defaults on corruption |
| Crash recovery | PID lock detection → recovery prompt |

## 15.6 Input Validation

| Input Surface | Validation |
|---|---|
| URL address bar | Sanitize input; reject `javascript:` and `data:` URLs (pass to Chromium's built-in security) |
| Settings values | Pydantic model validation with range checks, type checks, and enum constraints |
| Workspace names | Restrict to alphanumeric + space + hyphen + underscore; max 64 characters; filesystem-safe |
| Hotkey bindings | Validate modifier + key combination; reject reserved system hotkeys |
| Import files (.bmw) | Validate ZIP structure, schema versions, and file sizes before extraction |
| Clipboard text | Accept any Unicode text; no length limit enforced at input (soft limit managed at storage) |

## 15.7 Dependency Security

| Practice | Implementation |
|---|---|
| Pin all dependencies | `requirements.txt` with exact versions; no floating version specifiers |
| Audit dependencies | Run `pip-audit` in CI; fail on known vulnerabilities |
| Minimize dependencies | Only 6 runtime dependencies (PySide6, Pydantic, Loguru, pywin32, plus stdlib) |
| Supply chain verification | Verify package hashes with `pip install --require-hashes` |

## 15.8 Update Security

- Updates are checked via HTTPS only (TLS 1.2+).
- Update metadata includes a cryptographic hash (SHA-256) of the release archive.
- The application **does not** auto-update; it notifies the user and directs them to the download page.
- No code execution from the update mechanism.

## 15.9 Privacy Considerations

| Principle | Implementation |
|---|---|
| No telemetry | The application does not send any data to any server |
| No analytics | No usage tracking, crash reporting, or feature flags from external services |
| No accounts | No user accounts, no sign-up, no authentication with batmanoverlay servers |
| Local-only data | All data (clipboard, bookmarks, history, settings) stays in the local `data/` directory |
| No third-party SDKs | No advertising, analytics, or social SDKs |
| Clear data ownership | Users own their data; it's in plain files they can inspect, back up, or delete |

